{
  "walkthrough": [
    "Goal: week 2's perspective (V大's 透视盒子, with Drawabox's checks): complete a box in perspective from its front corner, draw ellipses that fit their frames, and draw boxes freely on a horizon; then see the geometry itself, the exact answer over my lines and my own edges drawn on to where they vanish, never a score.",
    "Entry (S101: the test crops imported first, so a combination copies a crop): in the gallery the Today card's options start Stage 2 · Single shapes. Without a pack of shapes a stage-2 session holds no shapes: a colour combination and a value scale, then its perspective in turn by session: in the first a box to complete and an ellipse, in the second a page of free boxes (the next sessions a three-point box and an ellipse in perspective, free boxes on a high horizon, and so on). I pass the colour work with Next.",
    "The box: in the left frame the horizon (labelled) and the box's front corner with its three edges (of a box across the horizon, the corner farther from it); the same in the empty frame beside it, where I draw. The first time, the instructions show by themselves: near is bigger, far is smaller; edges running the same way meet at one vanishing point, those parallel to the ground on the horizon; judge where the given edges meet (the points may lie off the frame); draw the other nine edges with Draw, the hidden ones too, each in one sure stroke, ghosted first; Sketch for construction lines; then Compare. The tools beside the frame: Sketch, Draw, Undo, Redo.",
    "Copy: a construction line with Sketch, then the nine edges with Draw, one of the bottom edges a little off.",
    "Compare: the exact box lies over mine in red, its two vanishing points ringed red off the frame; each of my edges is drawn on dashed towards its point, blue where it heads for it, red for the one I drew off, which misses its ring. The card: '8 of 9 edges head for their vanishing point.' No In place or Aligned: geometry has one answer. The pen is blue: I redraw the edge over the wrong one; the overlay goes as my pen lands; Compare again: the correction counts in place of the edge it redraws, 'All 9 edges head for their vanishing point.'. The left frame now shows the whole box, its hidden edges dashed, with its lines to its points, kept inside its frame.",
    "The ellipse: a plain frame (a table's cell) in both frames. Its instructions: draw the ellipse that touches the middle of each side; in perspective the middles lie nearer the far ends, found through the diagonals; round two or three times from the shoulder; Compare shows the exact one red, the one found in my lines dashed blue. I draw it twice round, a little narrow and to the left. Compare: the exact ellipse red with the four middles ringed, mine dashed blue; the card says 'Yours is flatter; it falls short of the right side and the left side'. The left frame now shows the exact ellipse with its diagonals and middle lines.",
    "Chinese: All tools, Actions, 中文: the card reads 椭圆 and its words in Chinese (更扁, 没碰到右边和左边). I draw the ellipse again over it in blue; Compare reads my correction alone: 你的椭圆很贴合. Finish: the summary in Chinese counts 透视盒子 and 椭圆 compared.",
    "Free boxes: One more starts the second session: a warm-up of the box and ellipses just met, then the colour work, then a page of free boxes on a middle horizon (视平线). Its instructions: three or four boxes freely, each turned another way and of other proportions; verticals vertical; each box's edges of a direction meet at one point on the horizon; front corner wider than 90°, about 120. I draw three boxes, every edge and the hidden ones: one well, one whose vanishing points sit too close (its bottom corner narrow), one with an edge turned off.",
    "Compare: every straight edge is drawn on dashed to the horizon, blue where it meets its direction's point (ringed blue), red for the edge turned off; a vertical that leaned would get its true vertical in red; the narrow front corner is marked with a red arc. The card: 3 个盒子，36 条边, one box whose edges of a direction don't meet, one front corner under 90° (what the red arc and the rings mean, How says). Back in English, the same in English.",
    "Not here: a pack's shapes in the same session (s80); boxes and ellipses on the tablet's own hand (the author's)."
  ],
